{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Any man, to all close flesh of his body, you shall not approach, etc.</b> This verse prohibits sexual relations with close flesh, and 'close flesh' is what the parashah enumerates one after another. For the perpetuation of the species does not come from close flesh, and the offspring is not viable; therefore the verse prohibits such intercourse. From here you learn that the matter of sexual relations is forbidden by Torah law except for the perpetuation of the species alone, and this is what is written (Leviticus 20:19): ״כי את שארו הערה״ — the verse teaches you that because of the close kinship, the matter is distanced. And the matter of the menstruant (niddah) that the Torah prohibited likewise indicates that it is not permitted except for the perpetuation of the species, for indeed the perpetuation of the species does not come from the blood of menstruation, since the fetus is formed from the woman's blood — either all of it or most of it — but from the blood of menstruation it would not be formed at all; thus wrote the Ramban, of blessed memory. And what shall the Master say regarding one with crushed testicles (פצוע דכא), that he is permitted to enter the congregation of HaShem — such as a convert and a freed woman — and this is a proof that whatever is not for the perpetuation of the species is not forbidden, as I shall explain when I reach that passage, with the help of Heaven." "<b>And behold,</b> the Rabad explained in his book, in the Gate of Holiness, that sexual relations are of five parts: the first, an obligation for the perpetuation of the species; the second, the commandment of conjugal duty (עונה); the third, for healing; the fourth, to escape from improper fantasy (הרהור); and the fifth, which is forbidden — for pleasure and lust, like the lust of beasts." "<b>And the Master,</b> of blessed memory, wrote in chapter 41 of The Guide of the Perplexed, that the reason for the prohibition of forbidden unions (עריות) is to diminish sexual relations and to render it repugnant, and all the forbidden unions with females are gathered under one principle, and that principle is that each of them is found, for the most part, with the man to whom she is forbidden, constantly in his house, and he is secluded with them; and such is the view of the sage Rabbi Abraham, of blessed memory." "<b>But</b> the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote that this is a very weak reason — that the verse should impose excision (כרת) upon these because they happen to be with him occasionally, while it would permit a man to marry many women, by the hundreds and thousands. And what harm would there be if he married his daughter alone, as is permitted among the children of Noah, and if he married two sisters as did Jacob our father? And a man would have no fitting marriages such as marrying his daughter to his son who is older than her, and settling them in his inheritance, and being fruitful and multiplying in his house — ״כי הארץ לא תהו בראה לשבת יצרה״ (for not as a void did He create it; He formed it to be inhabited). And we have nothing received by tradition in this matter, but according to reason there is in this matter a secret of the secrets of formation bound to the soul, and it belongs to the mystery of the transgression, to which we have already alluded — up to here are the words of the Master, of blessed memory." "<b>And I shall</b> explain to you the reason for the prohibition of forbidden unions (עריות). Know that kinship is of three categories: the first — kinship in which there was never any separation; the second — those separated who were never in kinship; the third — those close in flesh who had already been separated. Kinship in which there was never separation: they are permitted to have relations with one another, in the manner of the kinship of the first man with his sons and daughters, who were all permitted to have relations with one another, like a root that grasps its branches. And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: ״עלו למטה שנים וירדו שבעה — קין ותאומתו, הבל ושתי תאומותיו״ (they went down two and came up seven — Cain and his twin, Abel and his two twins). For the seven of them were a sign of unity, like the seven branches of the Menorah emerging from a single root; so too these offspring were from a single root and a single source, like Adam and Eve, who were two and were from one another. And concerning this the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: ״ראויה היתה לו בתו לאדם הראשון אלא שעשה חסד עם קין ונתן לו בתו״ — a daughter was fitting for the first man, but he performed kindness with Cain and gave him his daughter — as is said (Psalms 89:3): ״עולם חסד יבנה״. And this kinship, in which there was never separation, they were permitted one to another because their creation was as one, like a root in which all is unified. Those separated who were never in kinship: they too are permitted to have relations with one another, for since they were distant and separate, they are permitted one to another — like a branch cut from a tree that one plants to restore it to its root; so too those who are distant are fit to be united and to become a root. Those close ones who are too close: they are forbidden to have relations with one another because they had already been separated. And concerning this, this passage of forbidden unions was written, that the Torah prohibits those close ones who had already been separated prior to that kinship, for it is impossible for those separated to be a sign and token of unity, since there had already been separation between them. And the general principle: the distant ones were permitted to have relations with one another in order to show the signs of unity, and those too close were forbidden to have relations with one another because there had been separation between them, and in these offspring there are no signs of unity, since they were not created in a single creation, as was the case with Adam and Eve and their children." "<b>Lift up</b> your eyes and see: for the branches need to be joined with the root, and this is the reason for permitting the distant relatives; and likewise the root — that the branches be separated from it, for its excellence is not made known except through the branches, and this is the reason for the near relatives. And this is the reason for the parashah on the prohibition of forbidden relations (arayot): everyone who lies with them is as one who severs unity. Therefore HaShem — the unified One — is mentioned at the beginning of the parashah of arayot, as it says, <b>I am YHWH</b> — and understand this. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Sanhedrin: ״איש איש אל כל שאר בשרו״ — 'a man, a man' — to include the gentiles, who are admonished concerning arayot just as Israel is. And likewise (Leviticus 24:15): ״איש איש כי יקלל אלהיו״; (ibid. 24:20 — ed.) ״איש איש אשר יתן מזרעו למולך״ — to include the gentiles, who are admonished concerning the blasphemy of HaShem and concerning idolatry."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {3 "The phrase 'סוד העבור' (mystery of transgression) likely alludes to a kabbalistic matter discussed earlier in the commentary; rendered literally without expansion.", 5 "Second citation is marked '(שם כ)' i.e. ibid. 20; the verse about Molech is actually Leviticus 20:2, while 24:20 is the lex talionis passage — likely a citation error; rendered as written. Also, 'ושא עיניך' ('lift up your eyes') appears to be a proem-style opening not directly tied to the lemma of Leviticus 18:6; rendered faithfully as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:39.444749345Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}